    "text": "# Root-cause analysis: WAL-recovered data lost by a no-write `optimize()`\n\nAll findings below come from reading the frozen tree at `/workspace` (repo-relative paths). I did not build or execute anything; the final section separates source-established facts from execution-dependent ones.\n\n**One-line summary:** WAL replay rebuilds documents only in memory and never attaches the segment's `wal_file_` handle. `SegmentImpl::flush()` uses `wal_file_ == nullptr || !has_record()` as its \"nothing to persist\" test (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:2220`), so the `dump()` inside `optimize()` is a silent no-op \u2014 yet `switch_to_new_segment_for_writing()` still removes the writing-block metadata that is the only pointer to the WAL, and flushes that version to disk (`src/db/collection.cc:1538-1549`). The WAL is orphaned and the documents are unreachable at the next open.\n\n---\n\n## 1. State divergence between the recovery path and the ordinary insert path\n\n**Ordinary insert** (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:903-918`): `Insert()` calls `append_wal(doc)` first. `append_wal()` lazily calls `open_wal_file()` (`segment.cc:4324-4340`), which assigns the member `wal_file_` (`segment.cc:4303-4322`), then appends the record. `internal_insert()` then populates the memory components (`segment.cc:837-881`). So an inserted-into segment has **both** in-memory data **and** an attached WAL handle whose `has_record()` (file size > header, `src/db/index/storage/wal/local_wal_file.h:75-77`) is the dirty-flag that `flush()` keys on.\n\n**Recovery path**: `CollectionImpl::recovery()` reopens the version's writing segment (`src/db/collection.cc:1879-1887`); `SegmentImpl::Open` sees a `writing_forward_block` in the meta and calls `recover()` (`segment.cc:484-501`). `recover()` (`segment.cc:4193-4301`):\n\n- opens a **separate, read-only** handle `recover_wal_file` (4206-4215) and closes it on exit via `AILEGO_DEFER` (4215);\n- replays each record through `internal_insert/update/upsert/delete` (4249-4272) \u2014 correctly skipping `append_wal` so records aren't re-logged;\n- advances the writing-block range: `mem_block.max_doc_id_ += added_docs` (4285-4288).\n\nThe member `wal_file_` is **never assigned** \u2014 `open_wal_file()`'s only caller is `append_wal()`. This is the divergent state:\n\n| | after ordinary inserts | after pure WAL recovery |\n|---|---|---|\n| docs in memory components (`memory_store_`, indexers, `doc_ids_`) | yes | yes |\n| records in on-disk WAL | yes | yes |\n| `wal_file_` handle attached (flush's dirty signal) | **yes** | **no (nullptr)** |\n\n**Why the data is initially readable:** everything a reader touches is in RAM. `internal_insert` populated the forward memory store, vector/scalar indexers, `id_map_`, and `doc_ids_` (`segment.cc:837-881`). `SegmentImpl::doc_count()` counts `doc_ids_` (`segment.cc:589-599`); collection reads/stats use `get_all_segments()`, which includes the writing segment whenever `doc_count() > 0` (`src/db/collection.cc:2076-2082`). Durability at this point rests entirely on the on-disk WAL plus the version's writing-block pointer (the WAL path is derived from segment id + writing-block id, `segment.cc:4198-4199`).\n\n## 2. Call sequence and exact condition letting `optimize()` succeed while dropping the data\n\n1. `CollectionImpl::Optimize` (`src/db/collection.cc:779-805`): `writing_segment_->doc_count() != 0` is true (recovered docs counted), so it calls `switch_to_new_segment_for_writing()` (796).\n2. `switch_to_new_segment_for_writing` (`collection.cc:1517-1552`) calls `writing_segment_->dump()` (1519).\n3. `dump()` (`segment.cc:2197-2215`) calls `flush()`. **The exact condition** \u2014 `segment.cc:2220`:\n   ```cpp\n   if (wal_file_ == nullptr || !wal_file_->has_record()) {\n     return Status::OK();\n   }\n   ```\n   After pure recovery `wal_file_ == nullptr`, so `flush()` returns OK **without** doing any of: `memory_store_->flush()` (2233-2236), `finish_memory_components()` (2288-2291 \u2014 which writes the forward-block file and records persisted blocks in the segment meta, `segment.cc:4342-4412`), `update_version()` (2301), or WAL removal (2304-2315). `dump()` then just seals indexers and sets `sealed_ = true` (2204-2212), returning OK.\n4. `switch_to_new_segment_for_writing` proceeds **unconditionally**: adds the old segment to `segment_manager_` as if persisted (1522), creates a new empty writing segment (1527-1534), then:\n   - `writing_segment_meta->remove_writing_forward_block()` (**1538**) \u2014 deletes the only metadata from which the WAL path can be derived on reopen;\n   - `version.add_persisted_segment_meta(writing_segment_meta)` (1539) \u2014 declares the segment persisted even though its `persisted_blocks()` contain no block covering the recovered docs;\n   - `version_manager_->apply(version)` + `flush()` (1546-1549) \u2014 durably commits that false claim.\n5. Back in `Optimize`: `get_all_persist_segments()` (802-803) now includes the old segment; for one small segment `build_compact_task` emits a `CreateVectorIndexTask` (991-1002). `create_all_vector_index`/`create_vector_index` (`segment.cc:1617-1640`, `1670-1729`) may merge the **in-memory** vector indexers into a new on-disk index file and add a `VECTOR_INDEX` persisted block (1711-1727), or skip as already-indexed (1685-1690) \u2014 all return OK. Version apply + flush (871-877), `Status::OK()` returned (914). No forward (SCALAR) block for the recovered docs is ever written or recorded.\n6. **Reopen in a third process**: `CollectionImpl::recovery()` opens the old segment from `v.persisted_segment_metas()` read-only (`collection.cc:1861-1874`); `SegmentImpl::Open` finds no `writing_forward_block`, so `recover()` is **skipped** (`segment.cc:484-510`). The WAL file \u2014 still on disk, since the no-op flush never reached `wal_file_->remove()` (2305-2315) \u2014 is orphaned: nothing derives its path anymore. The persisted blocks don't cover the docs. They are gone.\n\n**Why the control case survives:** with same-process inserts, `wal_file_` is attached and `has_record()` true, so `dump()`\u2192`flush()` runs the full sequence: memory blocks written, `finish_memory_components()` records them in the meta, `update_version()` flushes, and only then is the WAL removed (`segment.cc:2224-2315`). The invariant \"WAL discarded only after the state it protects is persisted and versioned\" holds.\n\n## 3. Why OK returns and same-handle statistics prove nothing here\n\n- **The OK is real but meaningless**: every step genuinely succeeded; the defect is that `flush()`'s early-return *defines* \"nothing to persist\" as \"no WAL handle attached,\" conflating handle state with dirty state. No layer cross-checks that persisted blocks cover the writing block's doc range before `remove_writing_forward_block()` runs.\n- **Stats read live RAM, not disk**: `CollectionImpl::Stats()` (`collection.cc:403-442`) sums `segment->doc_count(...)` over `get_all_segments()`; the old segment object survives inside `segment_manager_` (added at `collection.cc:1522`) with its `doc_ids_` and memory store intact, so `doc_count` and `Fetch` keep succeeding in-process. Optimistically, `optimize` may even have written a merged vector index built from that in-memory data \u2014 partial persistence that makes success look credible while the forward data (the documents themselves) was never durably recorded.\n- Durability here is a property of **on-disk version + persisted blocks + WAL reachability**, and none of those is consulted by `Stats()` or by `Optimize`'s return path.\n\n## 4. Smallest repair principle and a regression test (described, not implemented)\n\n**Repair principle:** *recovery must restore the persistence obligation attached to the data, not just the data.* After `recover()`, the segment must be indistinguishable \u2014 for durability bookkeeping \u2014 from one that inserted the same docs through the normal path.\n\n**Smallest change:** at the end of `SegmentImpl::recover()` (`segment.cc:4193-4301`), call `open_wal_file()` to adopt the existing WAL as the member `wal_file_`. This is safe with the existing primitives: `LocalWalFile::open` with `create_new=false` opens the existing file and seeks to end for appending (`local_wal_file.cc:92-106`), and `has_record()` is size-based (`local_wal_file.h:75-77`), so it immediately reports true. The next `flush()`/`dump()` \u2014 including the one inside `optimize()` \u2014 then takes the full path: persist memory blocks, record them in the version, and only afterwards remove the WAL (`segment.cc:2288-2315`).\n\nOptional hardening (beyond the minimal fix): make `flush()`'s guard test actual in-memory dirtiness (e.g., `memory_store_ != nullptr` / writing-block `doc_count_ > 0`) rather than the WAL handle; and/or have `switch_to_new_segment_for_writing` refuse `remove_writing_forward_block()` unless the dump demonstrably sealed the segment's docs into persisted blocks.\n\n**Regression-test sequence** (mirrors the existing `tests/db/crash_recovery/` harness):\n\n1. **Process A**: create collection, insert N docs (N small enough to avoid rollover), exit abruptly without flush/close (the harness already has crash-driver tooling, e.g. `RunOptimizerAndCrash` used by `optimize_recovery_test.cc:167`).\n2. **Process B**: open the collection (WAL replay); assert `Stats().doc_count == N` and `Fetch` of all N pks.\n3. **Still in B, insert nothing** and run `optimize()`; assert OK (and optionally `Stats().doc_count == N` on the same handle).\n4. **Process C**: reopen; assert `Stats().doc_count == N`, `Fetch` returns all N docs identical, and a `Query` returns expected hits.\n\nAgainst the current source, step 4 should fail with the recovered docs missing; after the repair it must pass. A useful invariant assertion: after step 3, no WAL file exists for any segment whose persisted meta lacks a writing block (no orphaned WAL).\n\n**Why existing tests miss this:** `OptimizeRecoveryTest.CrashDuringOptimize` (`tests/db/crash_recovery/optimize_recovery_test.cc:144-261`) inserts 500 more batches *after* recovery (207-218) before invoking the optimizer (223). Those inserts go through `append_wal` \u2192 `open_wal_file`, attaching `wal_file_` and masking the no-op flush. The untested case is precisely *recover \u2192 optimize with zero intervening writes*.\n\n---\n\n### Established from source vs. requiring execution\n\n- **Established from source (read-only):** the entire causal chain \u2014 `recover()` never assigning `wal_file_`; the `segment.cc:2220` guard; `dump()`'s resulting no-op; `remove_writing_forward_block()` + version flush at `collection.cc:1538-1549`; recovery being gated on the writing block at `segment.cc:484-510`; stats/`doc_count` reading in-memory state; and the existing test's masking insertion.\n- **Would require executing a test:** end-to-end confirmation that `optimize()` returns OK for every schema variant (e.g., FTS `dump_fts_indexers`/`seal` behavior on a recovered segment), the precise reopen-time symptoms (whether `Fetch` by pk fails cleanly or finds stale id-map entries \u2014 `id_map_->flush()` at `segment.cc:2266-2269` is also skipped on the no-op path, and I did not fully trace IDMap write-through semantics), and validation of the proposed repair. I have not run any reproduction."
  },
